<GuideLayout title="How to Compare Mixed Units Without Misleading Math" description="Convert ounces and pounds, grams and kilograms, and milliliters and liters to a common basis with practical worked examples and clear limits.">
<p>Two prices are comparable only when their quantities describe the same kind of measurement on the same scale. A shelf label showing dollars per pound cannot be compared directly with one showing dollars per ounce. Converting first prevents a 16-to-1 scale difference from looking like a bargain.</p>
<h2>Ounces and pounds</h2><p>One pound contains 16 ounces. For 16 oz at $4.80 and 1.5 lb at $6.72, convert 1.5 × 16 = 24 oz. The first is $4.80 ÷ 16 = $0.30/oz. The second is $6.72 ÷ 24 = $0.28/oz, so the 1.5 lb package is lower by two cents per ounce.</p><p>You can instead convert 16 oz to 1 lb and compare $4.80/lb with $4.48/lb. Either common basis produces the same winner.</p>
<h2>Grams and kilograms</h2><p>One kilogram contains 1,000 grams. A 600 g bag at $3.90 costs $0.0065 per gram, or $0.65 per 100 g. A 1 kg bag at $6.20 costs $0.0062 per gram, or $0.62 per 100 g. Expressing both per 100 g makes the three-cent difference easier to read without changing it.</p>
<h2>Milliliters and liters</h2><p>One liter contains 1,000 mL. A 750 mL bottle at $3.15 costs $4.20/L. A 1.5 L bottle at $6 costs $4/L. The larger bottle is lower by 20 cents per liter. U.S. fluid ounces, cups, pints, quarts, and gallons can also be converted by volume; the calculator uses U.S. liquid measures.</p>
<h2>Measurements that should not be compared directly</h2><p>Weight ounces and fluid ounces are not interchangeable. Weight describes mass; fluid ounces describe volume. Converting between weight and volume would require a product’s density, which this site does not guess. A gram-versus-milliliter comparison has the same problem.</p><p>Counts also require meaning. One roll is not equal to one sheet, and one bottle is not a fixed volume. If two paper products list sheets per roll, compare sheets. If two drinks list bottle volume, compare volume.</p><p>U.S. and metric weights, and U.S. and metric lengths, are separate calculator groups. Convert externally only when the package facts give you a reliable basis. Never treat the numeric labels as comparable just because both are measurements.</p>
<h2>Enter mixed units in Compare Deals</h2><p>In <a href="/">Compare Deals</a>, choose the unit printed for each package. Compatible units normalize automatically, and incompatible categories produce a warning instead of a winner. The results display all prices using Deal A’s unit so the comparison stays on one scale.</p><p>Read the <a href="/methodology/">methodology</a> for the exact conversion factors and rounding rule, or see the <a href="/shopping-examples/">worked examples</a> for a 16 oz versus 1.5 lb calculation.</p>
